---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---
<Docs slug="masks">
  <p>
    A mask adjusts part of a photo instead of all of it: darken a bright sky,
    lift a face, warm one corner. Open a photo in the editor and choose
    <strong>Masks &amp; Cleanup</strong> from the icons on the right panel's
    edge. The page starts with a row of buttons, one for each kind of mask:
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>+ Linear</strong> (<kbd>M</kbd>) &mdash; a gradient. Drag on
      the photo from where the effect is full to where it fades out. Good
      for a sky or a foreground.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>+ Radial</strong> (<kbd>Shift+M</kbd>) &mdash; an ellipse.
      Drag out from its center, or click once for a default size. Hold
      <kbd>Shift</kbd> and scroll over its center to soften or harden the
      edge.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>+ Subject</strong> &mdash; finds the person or main subject
      and masks it for you. It only works in the Mac app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>+ Sky</strong> &mdash; finds the sky and masks it for you.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Subject and Sky have no handles to drag. The mask finds its area in the
    background and shows a spinner until it is done. While one is picked,
    the area it covers is tinted red on the photo. If it fails, its label
    shows a <strong>!</strong>; hold the pointer over it for the reason,
    then click <strong>Retry</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Adjust a mask</h2>
  <p>
    Each mask is listed by kind and number, such as <strong>Radial
    1</strong>. Click one to pick it and drag its handles on the photo. Each
    mask has its own Temp, Tint, Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows,
    Whites, Blacks and Saturation, added on top of the photo's own
    sliders. <strong>Invert</strong> swaps where the mask applies and where
    it doesn't, and <strong>Delete</strong> removes it. A photo holds up to
    16 masks.
  </p>

  <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
    <table class="lp-compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Key</th>
          <th scope="col">Does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td>
          <td>Put the mask tool down, or let go of the picked mask</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><kbd>Delete</kbd></td>
          <td>Delete the picked mask, not the photo</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><kbd>Cmd+Z</kbd></td>
          <td>Undo the last mask change; a whole drag is one step</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Touch Up</h2>
  <p>
    The same page holds <strong>Touch Up</strong> for removing spots. Turn it
    on (<kbd>Q</kbd>) and click a blemish to heal it, up to 64 spots per
    photo. <kbd>[</kbd> and <kbd>]</kbd> change the brush size.
  </p>

  <p>
    Masks are stored with the photo's other adjustments in the
    <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside it, and they appear in
    thumbnails and in every <a href="/docs/export.html">export</a>. If a
    Subject or Sky mask is still being found, export waits for it.
  </p>
</Docs>
